WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
How can fertilizer on land leave deep Bay animals without enough oxygen?

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.400
Watch one Chesapeake Bay cross-section.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.760
The answer begins far from the deep water.

4
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:17.120
The surface may look lively while animals below face a hidden shortage.

5
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:24.067
Rain carries some fertilizer into a creek, then a river.

6
00:00:25.387 --> 00:00:29.067
Fertilizer and manure contain nutrients that living things need.

7
00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.570
Wastewater and some air pollution add nutrients too.

8
00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:39.070
Nutrients help growth, but their effect depends on where they go and how much

9
00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:40.070
arrives.

10
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:44.853
Pond water on a sunny windowsill can turn green as algae grow.

11
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:49.773
In the Bay, extra nitrogen and phosphorus can feed fast growth.

12
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:52.793
A thick growth is an algae bloom.

13
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:57.833
More nutrients can support more algae, though conditions still matter.

14
00:00:59.113 --> 00:01:01.893
The green water can block sunlight from grasses below.

15
00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:04.817
Later, algae die and sink.

16
00:01:05.977 --> 00:01:09.337
The sinking material carries the next part of the story downward.

17
00:01:10.797 --> 00:01:15.097
Now predict: where will oxygen run low, near the surface or in deep water?

18
00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:23.313
Deep water runs low on oxygen.

19
00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:26.493
Bacteria gather on the dead algae.

20
00:01:27.793 --> 00:01:32.693
These decomposers break down dead matter and use dissolved oxygen while they work.

21
00:01:33.913 --> 00:01:37.693
The algae are food for decomposers, not direct oxygen thieves.

22
00:01:38.817 --> 00:01:43.057
Change one ecosystem part, and effects can spread through the system.

23
00:01:44.377 --> 00:01:49.437
Here, nutrients change algae, decomposition, oxygen, and animal habitat.

24
00:01:50.817 --> 00:01:54.277
Each step changes the conditions faced by the next living part.

25
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.840
Summer makes the deep loss harder to replace.

26
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.140
Fresher river water floats above saltier Bay water.

27
00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:04.560
The layers mix poorly.

28
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.140
Warm water also holds less oxygen.

29
00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:13.860
Algae fed by spring nutrients can keep sinking and decaying into summer.

30
00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:18.073
Fish and crabs often move away from low oxygen.

31
00:02:19.433 --> 00:02:21.193
Adult oysters are fixed in place.

32
00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:25.113
Animals that cannot leave may become sick or die.

33
00:02:26.533 --> 00:02:27.793
Bacteria still live there.

34
00:02:29.113 --> 00:02:33.073
Low oxygen shrinks the deep habitat available to many animals.

35
00:02:34.133 --> 00:02:40.073
This process is eutrophication: extra nutrients feed too much algae growth, which

36
00:02:40.073 --> 00:02:41.453
can lead to low oxygen.

37
00:02:42.773 --> 00:02:44.993
Fertilizer does not take oxygen itself.

38
00:02:46.117 --> 00:02:48.457
The effects travel through living connections.

39
00:02:49.757 --> 00:02:54.297
The word names the nutrient-driven process, not every patch of green water.

40
00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:00.380
A useful model follows the change from its starting point to each connected part.

41
00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:05.300
It also asks what could weaken, strengthen, or interrupt the path.

42
00:03:06.383 --> 00:03:10.703
That makes the model useful for evidence, not just memorizing arrows.

43
00:03:12.163 --> 00:03:15.583
Evidence can test each link instead of only the first and last.
